---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "body fortress super advanced whey protein powder",
      "body fortress super advanced chocolate whey protein powder",
      "body fortress protein powder",
      "body fortress whey protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Best Walmart Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-walmart-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Body Fortress Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      A 30 g scoop, added vitamin C, D and zinc, and one of the lowest prices per gram on the Walmart shelf. Here is what is in the tub, where to buy it, and who should pick something else.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#lineup">Flavors and Sizes</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Whey</a>
      <a href="#immune">The Immune Claim</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing Status</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#compare">Body Fortress vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Body Fortress Super Advanced Whey is a solid budget whey that is excellent value at Walmart and poor value at the Amazon prices we saw. Buy the 3.9 lb chocolate or vanilla tub in a store, count the added vitamins if you take a multivitamin, and look elsewhere if you need sport certification or want to avoid sucralose.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on Body Fortress
          </h2>

          <p>
            Body Fortress Super Advanced Whey is a budget, concentrate-led whey that does one thing well: it delivers a lot of protein per dollar when you buy it at the right store. Per the label at the time of writing, one 45 g scoop of chocolate gives 30 g of protein, 180 calories, 3 g of sugar and a dose of vitamins C, D and zinc that the brand markets as immune support. It is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and it is listed in NSF's dietary supplement registry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you buy it changes the verdict more than which flavor you pick. The 3.9 lb tub at Walmart worked out to just under $1 per 25 g of protein at the price we saw, which is among the cheapest name-brand whey on any shelf. The same tub on Amazon was listed at $81.42 when we checked, about $1.74 per 25 g, which is more than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard in its 5 lb size. Buying Body Fortress on Amazon at those prices makes little sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our ranking is simple. Buy the <strong>chocolate or vanilla Super Advanced Whey in the 3.9 lb tub</strong>, ideally at Walmart or another big-box store, if you want the cheapest 30 g shake from a brand you recognize. Buy the <strong>Super Advanced Isolate</strong> only if the regular tub bloats you, and read its ingredient list first, because it is not a pure whey isolate. Skip Body Fortress entirely if you are a drug-tested athlete, if you avoid artificial sweeteners, or if you already take a multivitamin with zinc and vitamin D and plan to drink two scoops a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            We rank Body Fortress on its label, its certification status and its cost per 25 g of protein. We have not tasted these products, and any comments on taste and mixing below are summarized buyer feedback. For the full shelf comparison, including Six Star, Equate and Gold Standard, see our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">best Walmart protein powder</a> guide, and for the cheapest options from any retailer, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Body Fortress Range: Flavors and Sizes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Body Fortress is a drugstore and big-box brand owned by 1440 Foods, the company behind Pure Protein and MET-Rx, according to the copyright line on the Body Fortress website. NSF's registry lists the manufacturer as Worldwide Sport Nutritional Supplements, Inc., with facilities in Anaheim, California and Jeffersonville, Indiana. The packaging claims it is America's number one muscle and strength development powder brand, citing IRI retail sales data. That tells you it sells a lot of tubs, not that it is the best product.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sizes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Super Advanced Whey, Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">45 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.78 lb, 3.9 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Super Advanced Whey, Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">44 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.74 lb, 3.9 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Super Advanced Whey, Strawberry / Cookies N Creme / Chocolate PB</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 45 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.78 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Super Advanced Isolate (Chocolate, Vanilla Creme)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 39 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Super Advanced Mass Gainer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several hundred per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Multi-scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures come from retailer copies of the nutrition panels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. The core <strong>Super Advanced Whey</strong> is sold in chocolate, vanilla, strawberry, cookies n creme and chocolate peanut butter, which matches the five flavors NSF lists for both manufacturing facilities. Chocolate and vanilla are the only two sold in the big 3.9 lb tub at most retailers, so they are the flavors that actually deliver the low price per gram. The specialty flavors come mainly in the 1.78 lb size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The front of the tub says 60 g of protein, which confuses a lot of buyers. That figure is for two scoops. The label gives both a one-scoop column (45 g of powder, 30 g of protein, 180 calories) and a two-scoop column (90 g of powder, 60 g of protein, 360 calories), and the 1.78 lb tub holds 18 single scoops or 9 double servings. For most people, one scoop is a sensible serving. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein calculator guide</a> explains how much you need across a whole day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Super Advanced Isolate</strong> is the leaner product, at roughly 30 g of protein and 150 calories per scoop with no sugar, per retailer labels. The <strong>Mass Gainer</strong> is a different animal: a high-calorie, carbohydrate-heavy powder for people struggling to eat enough, with multi-scoop servings and nutrition figures that have changed between versions, so check the panel on the tub. The brand also sells creatine, glutamine and a pre-workout. This review focuses on the whey, which is what most people searching the brand want to buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Body Fortress Powders Worth Buying, Plus Two Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three Body Fortress products, flagship first, plus a better-tested and a leaner alternative for anyone buying on Amazon. Label figures are per retailer copies of the nutrition panels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw and change often. We have not tasted these products, and any taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Super Advanced Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            The chocolate label lists a super whey protein blend of whey protein concentrate and whey protein isolate, then cocoa processed with alkali, maltodextrin, lecithin, natural flavor, salt, cellulose gum, ascorbic acid (vitamin C), acesulfame potassium, sucralose, zinc oxide and vitamin D3. Vanilla is similar, minus the cocoa and with natural and artificial flavors. The label states that it contains milk and soy, and the soy comes from the lecithin. It is labeled gluten-free and aspartame-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            So what is the whey blend? Ingredients are listed by weight, and concentrate comes first, so this is a concentrate-led whey with some isolate added. Concentrate is the cheaper form and keeps more lactose, fat and cholesterol than isolate. That shows up on the panel: a scoop of chocolate has 4 g of fat, 2.5 g of it saturated, and 105 mg of cholesterol, about 35% of the daily value. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains why that matters if dairy sometimes upsets your stomach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maltodextrin is the other ingredient worth noticing. It is a cheap carbohydrate filler and thickener, and it sits third on the chocolate label, ahead of the flavorings. Combined with the concentrate, it explains why the scoop is so big. A 45 g scoop yielding 30 g of protein means the powder is about 67% protein by weight. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is about 77% (24 g in a 31 g scoop) and Premier Protein about 73% (30 g in 41 g), per their labels. Body Fortress is not short on protein, but you are buying more filler per tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The nutrition numbers are otherwise reasonable. Per scoop of chocolate, the label lists 7 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of fiber, 3 g of sugar, 230 mg of sodium and 310 mg of potassium, plus 140 mg of calcium. It also lists 6 g of BCAAs and about 3.1 g of leucine per scoop, which is a good leucine dose and comfortably above the 2-3 g per meal that research links to a strong muscle-building response. On protein quality, Body Fortress is perfectly competent.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LABEL CHECK</p>
