        <div id="immune" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Immune Support Claim, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The selling point printed on the current tubs is immune support from vitamins C and D plus zinc. Per the label, one scoop contains 90 mg of vitamin C (100% of the daily value), 25 mcg of vitamin D (1,000 IU, 125% of the daily value) and 11 mg of zinc (100% of the daily value). A two-scoop serving doubles all three, to 200%, 250% and 200% of the daily value. The tub carries the standard FDA disclaimer that these statements have not been evaluated.
          </p>

          <p>
            The claim itself is a structure and function claim, and it is accurate in the narrow sense that all three nutrients play a role in normal immune function. The catch is that the benefit is clearest in people who are low in them. If you are deficient in vitamin D or zinc, getting more helps. If you already eat a varied diet or take a multivitamin, adding more on top has much weaker evidence. Research on regular vitamin C supplements finds a small reduction in how long colds last, not fewer colds for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more practical issue is stacking. The tolerable upper intake level for adults is 40 mg of zinc and 100 mcg (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day. Two scoops of Body Fortress give 22 mg of zinc and 50 mcg of vitamin D. Add a multivitamin with 11 mg of zinc and 25 mcg of vitamin D, plus zinc lozenges at the first sign of a cold, and you can get close to or over the zinc limit. Long-term high zinc intake can interfere with copper absorption.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of that is a reason to fear the product. One scoop a day alongside a normal diet is well within safe limits for healthy adults. It is a reason to count what you already take. If you take a vitamin D prescription, have kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication that interacts with zinc, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian first. The label itself says the product is not intended for pregnant or nursing women, anyone under 18 or people with kidney disease.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our take is that the vitamins are a marketing differentiator, not a reason to choose this powder. Vitamin C, vitamin D and zinc are cheap to add and cheap to buy separately. If you would otherwise buy a plain whey, the vitamins in Body Fortress do not justify paying more for it, and on Amazon you would be paying more. Choose it on price per gram, and treat the vitamins as a small extra you need to account for.
          </p>
        </div>

