          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Amazon without checking the price per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The same 3.9 lb chocolate tub cost $45.28 on Walmart's listing and $81.42 on Amazon when we checked. At the Amazon price, Gold Standard was cheaper per gram. Divide price by servings, then by protein per serving, and multiply by 25 before you buy, and check who the seller is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the 60 g front-label claim as one scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 60 g of protein printed on the tub is for two scoops, or 90 g of powder and 360 calories. One scoop gives 30 g of protein and 180 calories. If you have been using two scoops because of the front label, you may be drinking twice the calories you planned and emptying the tub in half the time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking the vitamins without counting them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two scoops give 22 mg of zinc and 50 mcg of vitamin D. Add a multivitamin and zinc lozenges and you can approach or pass the 40 mg adult upper limit for zinc. One scoop a day is fine for most healthy adults, but add up every supplement you take, and ask a doctor if you are unsure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming NSF listed means safe for drug-tested sport</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Body Fortress appears in NSF's registry under the contents standard, NSF/ANSI 173, which checks label accuracy and contaminants. We could not find it in NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which screen for banned substances. Tested athletes should choose a powder with a sport-level certification and keep the batch number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the isolate expecting pure whey isolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Super Advanced Isolate uses a blend of hydrolyzed whey isolate, milk protein isolate and whey isolate. Milk protein isolate contains casein and some lactose, so it is not a pure whey isolate. It still suits many lactose-sensitive buyers, but it is a dairy product and is never suitable for anyone with a milk allergy.
              </p>
